            <ArticleTitle>[Functional outcome of arthroscopic rotator cuff repair].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>620-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1556/OH.2014.29862</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Functional outcome after rotator cuff reconstruction is influenced by several factors of which re-rupture is probably the most important.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of the study was to evaluate the postoperative outcome including re-rupture rate after arthroscopic rotator cuff reconstruction.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">23 shoulders of 22 patients were examined prospectively. Physical examination, ultrasound and radiography were performed. Quality of life and functional outcome were evaluated using Constant Score and Visual Analog Scale.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Excellent or good results were found in 80% of the patients. The Constant Score has increased from 45 to 79, and the level of pain decreased from 6.6 to 2.5. Full-thickness rotator cuff tear was absent, but partial tear occurred in 7 cases (30%). Average acromiohumeral distance in the operated side was 8.5 mm compared to 9.5 mm measured on the contralateral shoulder.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Arthroscopic rotator cuff repair is a safe and reliable procedure that provides good results.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
